{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"3982b9e0b75f10b6","name":"card_event_pound","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_pound","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"POUND","text":"Deal damage to a ground unit equal to the total attack of your air units.","flavorText":"RAF Fighter Command played a crucial role in defending Britain and maintaining air superiority during WWII. Formed in 1936, it rose to prominence during the Battle of Britain in 1940 when it repelled the Luftwaffe's efforts to gain control of British airspace. Using a network of radar stations and ground observers, Fighter Command coordinated quick responses to incoming raids. Its primary aircraft, the Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane, helped secure victory despite being outnumbered. After the battle, Fighter Command shifted to offensive operations over occupied Europe, escorting bombers and carrying out fighter sweeps. It also adapted to changing needs, eventually forming part of the 2nd Tactical Air Force to support ground operations after the Normandy landings. By the war's end, it had flown hundreds of thousands of sorties and claimed over 15,000 enemy aircraft. Fighter Command’s integration of radar, flexible tactics, and skilled pilots made it a model for modern air defense. Its success in 1940 prevented a German invasion and ensured Britain remained a base for Allied operations throughout the war.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_pound","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_pound_title","SourceString":"POUND","LocalizedString":"POUND"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_pound_text","SourceString":"Deal damage to a ground unit equal to the total attack of your air units.","LocalizedString":"Deal damage to a ground unit equal to the total attack of your air units."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_pound_history","SourceString":"RAF Fighter Command played a crucial role in defending Britain and maintaining air superiority during WWII. Formed in 1936, it rose to prominence during the Battle of Britain in 1940 when it repelled the Luftwaffe's efforts to gain control of British airspace. Using a network of radar stations and ground observers, Fighter Command coordinated quick responses to incoming raids. Its primary aircraft, the Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane, helped secure victory despite being outnumbered. After the battle, Fighter Command shifted to offensive operations over occupied Europe, escorting bombers and carrying out fighter sweeps. It also adapted to changing needs, eventually forming part of the 2nd Tactical Air Force to support ground operations after the Normandy landings. By the war's end, it had flown hundreds of thousands of sorties and claimed over 15,000 enemy aircraft. Fighter Command’s integration of radar, flexible tactics, and skilled pilots made it a model for modern air defense. Its success in 1940 prevented a German invasion and ensured Britain remained a base for Allied operations throughout the war.","LocalizedString":"RAF Fighter Command played a crucial role in defending Britain and maintaining air superiority during WWII. Formed in 1936, it rose to prominence during the Battle of Britain in 1940 when it repelled the Luftwaffe's efforts to gain control of British airspace. Using a network of radar stations and ground observers, Fighter Command coordinated quick responses to incoming raids. Its primary aircraft, the Supermarine Spitfire and Hawker Hurricane, helped secure victory despite being outnumbered. After the battle, Fighter Command shifted to offensive operations over occupied Europe, escorting bombers and carrying out fighter sweeps. It also adapted to changing needs, eventually forming part of the 2nd Tactical Air Force to support ground operations after the Normandy landings. By the war's end, it had flown hundreds of thousands of sorties and claimed over 15,000 enemy aircraft. Fighter Command’s integration of radar, flexible tactics, and skilled pilots made it a model for modern air defense. Its success in 1940 prevented a German invasion and ensured Britain remained a base for Allied operations throughout the war."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/World_at_War/T_pound.T_pound","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/POUND.POUND","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":366.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_pound_photo","SourceString":"Hurricane Mk Is of No. 245 Squadron over Northern Ireland in November 1940.","LocalizedString":"Hurricane Mk Is of No. 245 Squadron over Northern Ireland in November 1940."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"totalAirAttack","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_pound_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasOutParms | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"CallFunc_GetTargetedCard_hasTarget","type":